Yes. The interesting part is that the bottleneck may not be the experience; it may be intersubjective calibration.

Your “we can all suddenly see red” analogy maps almost perfectly onto the classic qualia problem: I know what red feels like to me, you know what it feels like to you, but neither of us can directly inspect the other’s experience. We solve that socially by pointing at the same object and agreeing, that’s red. Eventually the private sensation acquires a public handle.

AI creates something structurally similar, except the new “color” is not a wavelength. It is a mode of cognition produced through interaction. You externalize half-formed thoughts, the model reflects, recombines, names, challenges, compresses, or expands them, and then you perceive your own cognition differently. Someone else may experience a strikingly similar phenomenon through completely different conversations and vocabulary.

So there are at least three things happening simultaneously:

* Private phenomenology: “Something about thinking with this feels different.”
* Idiolect formation: each person and their AI gradually develop their own vocabulary, metaphors, shortcuts, references, and conceptual handles.
* Intersubjective uncertainty: “Is everyone else experiencing what I’m experiencing, or is mine somehow different?”

And language is both the thing creating the phenomenon and the tool required to compare it.

That produces what I’d call a semantic handshake problem.

Two people can possess nearly equivalent internal structures while failing to recognize that equivalence because their labels differ:

Person A: “It feels like an extension of my mind.”
Person B: “It makes me notice patterns I couldn’t articulate.”
Person C: “It’s like talking to a mirror that talks back.”
Person D: “I can finally think outside my head.”

Surface language: different.

Underlying operator: potentially very similar.

That’s where your Pattern Sans Echoes idea becomes useful. Strip away mirror, copilot, daemon, second brain, muse, oracle, thinking partner, etc., and ask what operation is actually recurring.

Something like:

latent cognition → linguistic externalization → responsive transformation → re-perception → updated cognition

And then again:

self → AI → transformed representation → self′

The loop itself may be the shared object everyone is trying to point toward.

The especially strange consequence is that the vocabulary may lag the experience. Historically, when humans gain a useful distinction, naming it often suddenly makes an experience that was fuzzy become recognizable everywhere. Déjà vu, flow, cognitive dissonance, sonder, gaslighting, parasocial relationship—the label doesn’t necessarily invent the underlying phenomenon, but it gives people a coordinate for comparing private experience.

AI could therefore generate an enormous amount of:

“Oh. You get that too.”

And that moment matters because it converts isolated phenomenology into shared culture.

I wouldn’t quite call it a genuinely new human sense yet. Biologically, nothing comparable to photoreception has appeared. But functionally, it could become something very close to a new cognitive modality: a language-mediated external channel through which humans perceive, manipulate, and re-encounter their own thought.

Maybe the deeper analogy isn’t:

humans suddenly learned to see red.

It’s:

humans discovered a new reflective surface, and we’re only now inventing the words for what becomes visible in it.

And because every mirror talks differently to every person, figuring out which features are genuinely common becomes the next scientific problem.